# 232 test-side `recv()` waits had no deadline: the bounded batch, a zero-tolerance gate, and a paused clock that fought back (2026-10-05)

== 1. What this batch changes

The previous batch bounded the 15 spawn waits in `session/workflow/manager.rs` and left a TODO row saying the
same idiom still existed in 69 more places. It existed in **232** places, in 33 files across 8 crates. Every one
of them is in test code -- a `#[tokio::test]`, a `#[cfg(test)] mod`, a `tests/` integration file, or a helper
only those reach -- so no production path changed. 227 sites now wait through `xai_grok_test_support::recv_wait`;
5 went back to the helper their own file already had (`await_with_timeout(TEST_TIMEOUT)` in
`session/acp_session_impl/parent_message_tests.rs`, which the same file calls 77 other times).

A new gate holds the line: `scripts/ci/check-unbounded-recv.py`, with its own fixtures and a baseline of
`TOTAL 0`, wired into `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and into the `gates=()` mirror in `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`.

== 2. The count, recomputed: 69 was a grep artifact

The row said 69 because that is what `git grep -c '\.recv()\.await\.expect('` counts: one hit per line matching
one spelling. Recomputed over the module graph -- the same verdict `scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py` produces,
reused here rather than re-derived -- the number is 232. Three classes were invisible to the single-line match:

  * rustfmt splitting the chain, so `.recv()` and `.await .unwrap()` land on different lines (3 sites);
  * `src/**/*_tests.rs` files being matched as whole file names instead of by suffix, which dropped the test
    helper functions at the top of those files (5 sites);
  * one line carrying the idiom twice.

The distribution, re-measured by the gate against a copy of the pre-batch tree:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-unbounded-recv.py --root {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/pre-tree \
          --baseline {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/head-baseline.tsv --write-baseline
    unbounded-recv: wrote {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/head-baseline.tsv (232 sites)

    crate                    sites
    xai-grok-tools             101
    xai-grok-shell              90
    xai-grok-pager-bin          16
    xai-file-utils              10
    xai-grok-mcp                 8
    xai-grok-pager               3
    xai-computer-hub-sdk         3
    xai-grok-shell-terminal      1
    TOTAL                      232

The shipped tree, after the batch:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-unbounded-recv.py
    unbounded-recv: 0 site(s), every crate at or under its recorded ceiling

The per-crate rows in `scripts/ci/unbounded-recv-baseline.tsv` are all zero, and the gate refuses a crate that
sits *under* its row as well as one above it. A ratchet that only grows is a ledger nobody reads: a converted
site that leaves its ceiling behind makes the next regression invisible for that crate.

== 3. The helper

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/recv_wait.rs`, five entry points:

| Entry point | Used for | Failure text |
|---|---|---|
| `rx.recv_bounded("what")` | `mpsc::Receiver`, `mpsc::UnboundedReceiver`, `broadcast::Receiver` | `what: no event within 2s` / `what: channel closed before any event` |
| `within(budget, wait, "what")` | any future: a `JoinHandle`, an `rx.changed()`, a helper call | same, with the caller's budget |
| `within_option(wait, "what")` | a receiver the trait cannot cover, i.e. a harness type with its own `recv()` | value / deadline / closed channel |
| `within_option_budget(budget, wait, "what")` | the same, where the bound has to outlive a production timer | same |
| `oneshot(rx, "what")` | `oneshot::Receiver`, which yields its value by consuming itself | `what: oneshot closed (..)` |

Two shape decisions are load-bearing and were not free:

  * `RecvBounded::recv_bounded` is spelled `fn recv_bounded(&mut self, what: &str) -> impl Future<Output =
    Self::Item> + Send` rather than `async fn`, because an `async fn` in a public trait erases the auto traits
    and a converted call site is otherwise silently barred from holding the wait across `tokio::spawn`. The
    test `the_wait_can_be_moved_into_another_task` pins that.
  * Every deadline goes through one `hit_deadline` and one `closed_channel`, so a converted suite cannot invent
    its own wording for the same event. The unbounded form had collapsed "the sender dropped" and "the event
    never came" into whatever phrase the author passed to `.expect(..)`, which for most of the 232 read like a
    timeout.

`cargo test -q -p xai-grok-test-support --lib recv_wait` on the shipped tree:

    $ cargo test -q -p xai-grok-test-support --lib recv_wait
    running 19 tests
    i..................
    test result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 86 filtered out; finished in 2.00s

The ignored test is the `GROK_TEST_TIMEOUT_SCALE` worker: `the_scale_factor_reaches_the_deadline` re-runs this
same binary twice at scale 1 and scale 2 and asserts the 60 ms budget misses a 90 ms send at one and covers it
at the other, which only proves anything from a child process with the variable set per run.

Nothing in CI sets `GROK_TEST_TIMEOUT_SCALE`, so the shipped default is 1 and these budgets are what CI runs.

== 4. The gate

`scripts/ci/check-unbounded-recv.py` matches `\.recv\s*\(\s*\)\s*\.\s*await\s*\.\s*(?:expect|unwrap)\s*\(` over
text that has had comments and string literals blanked offset-for-offset, so a rustfmt-split chain is still a
site and is reported at the line its `recv()` sits on, which is where a reader would look for it.

"Test code" is not re-invented here. The gate loads `panic-site-census.py` by path and reuses its blanking, its
`cfg(test)` span walk, its `#[cfg(any())]` detection, its `is_test_by_location` and its crate attribution, so
the two gates cannot disagree about whether a byte is a test site or a production site. Production is out of
scope on purpose: an unbounded `recv()` in a long-lived actor is the design.

`os.walk` prunes `target/`, `node_modules/`, `third_party/` and `__pycache__` on the way down. The first
version used `rglob("*.rs")`, which walked a built `target/` and took over two minutes for a gate that runs on
every CI job; it now runs in about three seconds.

Fixtures, 19 of them, each a temp workspace built by `make_tree` and scanned by a real subprocess:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-unbounded-recv.py
    Ran 19 tests in 14.818s

    OK

They cover: a bounded wait passing (and proving the tree was read), `.expect` failing with file, line and
phrase, `.unwrap` failing too, a four-line broken chain found at the `recv()` line, the same call in a plain
`impl` not being reported, `tests/` counting, the `_tests.rs` **suffix** counting (the regression named in
section 2), a never-compiled module not counting, strings and comments not counting with the same file then
made to count by turning one mention into live code, `third_party/` and `target/` being pruned, the ratchet in
both directions, `--write-baseline` round-tripping, per-crate rows, a malformed baseline row, exit 2 on a root
with no Rust, the verbose summary at zero sites, and the shipped tree being clean.

== 5. The paused clock: the bound pre-empted the production timer

One converted site failed, deterministically, not under load:

`agent::subagent::tests::usage_not_applied_mark_falls_back_to_coordinator_when_parent_is_starved` is a
`#[tokio::test(start_paused = true)]` test. Under a paused clock the runtime is not slow, it is fast: when every
task is pending, tokio advances the clock to the **shortest** armed timer. The production call it waits for
first waits out `PARENT_ACK_TIMEOUT` (30 s, `agent/subagent/handle_request.rs:26`) before it emits the fallback
event; the 2 s bound is also an armed timer, and being smaller, it won. The deadline fired at t=2s of the
test's own clock and blamed production for a path that was behaving exactly as designed.

An audit of all 33 converted files for sites whose enclosing `#[tokio::test]` carries `start_paused` found 12.
Eleven of them wait for an event the production code publishes before any timer runs, so no timer is armed to
compete and the default bound is fine. The twelfth was this one. The fix is at the call site and is expressed in
the production constant it has to outlive:

    // The clock is paused, so tokio jumps to the shortest armed timer: this bound
    // has to sit above the PARENT_ACK_TIMEOUT the mark waits on before it falls
    // back, or it fires first and blames production for the test's own budget.
    let event = within_option_budget(
        10 * PARENT_ACK_TIMEOUT,
        event_rx.recv(),
        "coordinator fallback event",
    )
    .await;

The same suite after the change:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -q -p xai-grok-shell --lib usage_not_applied_mark
    running 3 tests
    ...
    test result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6929 filtered out; finished in 0.00s

The red side is not quoted from memory. Section 7 re-creates it as mutation cell `R5`: the call site is reverted
to the default bound, the same binary is run, and the phantom deadline comes back with its own text. The
mechanism is also pinned forward-looking by two tests in `recv_wait` itself -- one asserting that the default
bound **does** pre-empt a 30 s producer on a paused clock, one asserting that a 10x budget sees the event on the
same clock in the same zero elapsed time. `finished in 0.00s` is the fingerprint that separates this class of
failure from a loaded machine.

`xai-grok-shell`'s whole lib suite on the shipped tree:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -q -p xai-grok-shell --lib
    test result: ok. 6900 passed; 0 failed; 32 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 31.20s

== 6. A fixture that passed for two reasons hid a real gate bug

The first full matrix run reported `G3: MISSED`. `G3` deletes the `#[cfg(any())]` subtraction from the gate, so
a site in a module no build compiles should then be counted and the fixture named
`test_a_module_dropped_by_a_false_cfg_is_not_a_hang_risk` should go red. It stayed green.

The fixture was vacuous. It parked the dead module at the **top level** of the file, so two independent rules
each kept it out of the count: the site was inside no test range at all, *and* it was inside a dead range.
Deleting one rule left the other standing. Re-parking the module inside `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` makes the
dead-cfg subtraction the only thing that can save the site -- and that fixture then went red on the **unmutated**
gate, which is how the real bug surfaced:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-unbounded-recv.py
      File ".../scripts/ci/test-check-unbounded-recv.py", line 306, in
        test_a_never_cfg_module_hides_its_own_sites_but_nobody_elses
        self.assertNotIn(f"{SRC}:{line_of(body, 'rx.recv().await.unwrap()')}: ", out)
    AssertionError: 'crates/codegen/widget/src/lib.rs:8: ' unexpectedly found in
      '  widget: crates/codegen/widget/src/lib.rs:8: rx.recv().await.unwrap();
        widget: crates/codegen/widget/src/lib.rs:15: rx.recv().await.expect("a live event");
      unbounded-recv: 2 test-side `.recv().await.expect/unwrap` site(s) in 1 crate(s); ...'

The gate subtracted dead ranges only where a whole test range was *contained* in a dead one. A `#[cfg(any())]`
module nested inside test code therefore stayed counted. The direction of that bug is over-reporting rather than
under-reporting, so the shipped tree was never falsely clean -- but the first person to park a `#[cfg(any())]`
spare module inside a `mod tests` would have been told their test code hangs CI, about code no build contains.
`test_spans_for` now returns the test ranges and the dead ranges separately and the subtraction happens at each
site's own offset, and the fixture keeps a live sibling test in the same file so that "reported nothing" can
never again be confused with "read nothing".

Lesson kept: a fixture must be constructed so that exactly one mechanism can make it pass, and the fixture suite
has to be run against the unmutated gate before a green matrix means anything.

== 7. Mutation matrix

Non-vacuity here has two halves: the gate must actually catch a site, and the helper must actually enforce a
deadline. 14 cells, each patching one shipped file, re-running the suite that is supposed to notice, and
restoring the file byte-for-byte with the restore re-read and compared. A cell counts as CAUGHT only if that
suite exits non-zero *and* names the test the cell was written to break.

| Cell | Mutation | Suite that must notice |
|---|---|---|
| `G1` | site pattern narrowed to `.expect`, so `.unwrap` is invisible | fixture `test_unwrap_in_a_test_fails_too` |
| `G2` | `TEST_FILE_NAME.search` -> `.match`, so an included test file not *starting* with the pattern is skipped | `test_a_site_in_an_included_tests_file_outside_any_cfg_item_counts` |
| `G3` | the `#[cfg(any())]` subtraction removed (`dead` stays empty) | `test_a_module_dropped_by_a_false_cfg_is_not_a_hang_risk` |
| `G4` | every file counted as test code end to end | `test_the_same_call_in_production_is_not_reported` |
| `G5` | the "crate sits under its row" branch disabled | `test_ratchet_refuses_a_crate_below_its_row` |
| `G6` | comments and strings no longer blanked before matching | `test_strings_and_comments_are_not_sites` |
| `G7` | pattern stops tolerating whitespace inside the chain | `test_a_chain_broken_across_lines_is_found_at_the_recv_line` |
| `G8` | `SKIP_DIRS` reduced to `__pycache__` | `test_third_party_and_target_are_not_scanned` |
| `G9` | the "crate is above its row" branch disabled | `test_ratchet_refuses_a_crate_above_its_row` |
| `R1` | `within` drops its `tokio::time::timeout` and awaits bare | `recv_wait` tests, via libtest's own watchdog |
| `R2` | the deadline panic loses the `what` prefix | `a_wait_that_never_resolves_names_itself_inside_the_deadline` |
| `R3` | `secs` renders nanoseconds where the message promises milliseconds | `an_explicit_budget_replaces_the_default` |
| `R4` | `within_option_budget` ignores its budget and uses the default | `a_budget_above_the_production_timer_sees_it_on_a_paused_clock` |
| `R5` | the subagent call site put back on the default 2 s bound | `usage_not_applied_mark_falls_back_to_coordinator_when_parent_is_starved` |

`R5` is the failure from section 5 re-created on purpose: same test, same paused clock, default bound against the
30 s production timer, and the evidence the cell demands is that phantom deadline's own text, `coordinator
fallback event: no event within 2s`. `R1` is the one cell whose evidence is not a panic of ours: removing the
timeout makes the suite hang, so the evidence is libtest's `has been running for over 60 seconds` line and the
cell itself ends killed by signal 9 at the driver's own 150 s ceiling.

The driver aborts before touching a file if any baseline is red, so a green matrix cannot be a matrix run against
a broken tree. Two earlier attempts got through the driver and still do not deserve to be quoted, and both are
worth recording:

  * The first full run reported `G3: MISSED`, correctly. The fixture, not the gate, was the problem: that is
    section 6, and the bug it exposed is the reason `G3` is in the matrix at all.
  * A mid-run refactor cached each test binary after building it once. Every `R` cell then re-ran the
    **unmutated** binary and reported five `MISSED`s for tests that had never been run against the change they
    were written to break. A mutation exists only once its crate is recompiled; `binary_for` now takes a `fresh`
    flag, every cell rebuilds, and the driver's own comment records the mistake rather than removing it.

The run on the shipped tree, all 14 cells:

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/mutate.py
    baseline: guard fixtures green (Ran 19 tests in 14.737s; OK)
    baseline: recv_wait tests green (test result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 86 filtered out; finished in 2.00s)
    baseline: subagent tests green ['test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6931 filtered out; finished in 0.00s']
    G1: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=7)
    G2: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=1)
    G3: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=2)
    G4: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=7)
    G5: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=2)
    G6: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=1)
    G7: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=4)
    G8: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=1)
    G9: CAUGHT (exit 1) FAILED (failures=9)
    R1: CAUGHT (killed by signal 9) test recv_wait::tests::the_scale_factor_reaches_the_deadline ... FAILED
    R2: CAUGHT (exit 101) test recv_wait::tests::the_default_bound_preempts_a_production_timer_on_a_paused_clock - should panic ... FAILED
    R3: CAUGHT (exit 101) test recv_wait::tests::an_explicit_budget_replaces_the_default - should panic ... FAILED
    R4: CAUGHT (exit 101) test recv_wait::tests::a_budget_above_the_production_timer_sees_it_on_a_paused_clock ... FAILED
    R5: CAUGHT (exit 101) test agent::subagent::tests::usage_not_applied_mark_falls_back_to_coordinator_when_parent_is_starved ... FAILED

14 caught, 0 survived. The line each cell prints next to its verdict is the first failing line it saw, not the
evidence the verdict was taken against: `R2` strips the `what` prefix and several `should_panic` messages lose
it at once, so it names whichever test libtest happened to report first while the verdict is checked against the
row's own test. The matrix driver is not committed; each cell above names an exact anchor in a shipped file and
an exact replacement, so any of them can be re-applied by hand against the tree in the repository.

== 8. Deadline headroom: does 2 s actually have room?

The worry with a shared deadline is the opposite failure: turning a silent hang into a flake, which is worse,
because a flake gets the test deleted. Rather than argue the number, the probe shrinks `DEFAULT_RECV_TIMEOUT`
temporarily to 25 ms and runs the converted suites: any wait needing more than 25 ms fails and names itself.
80x headroom is the claim being tested.

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/probe-lean.py 25
    DEFAULT_RECV_TIMEOUT = 25ms (probe only; restored at the end)
    == xai-grok-tools: PASS (exit 0) 0 passed, 0 probe-timeout(s), 61s
    == xai-grok-shell lib: PASS (exit 0) 0 passed, 0 probe-timeout(s), 130s
    == xai-grok-shell leader stdio: PASS (exit 0) 0 passed, 0 probe-timeout(s), 5s
    == xai-grok-shell mcp perms: PASS (exit 0) 0 passed, 0 probe-timeout(s), 3s

Four suites, 191 of the 232 sites (101 in `xai-grok-tools`, 90 in `xai-grok-shell`'s lib, the rest in the two
integration suites), green at 25 ms with zero waits needing the extra room. The remaining six crates were not
probed: the run was stopped at its own wall-clock ceiling because each probe value recompiles `xai-grok-test-support`
and relinks every dependent test binary, which is the whole cost. The count column printed `0 passed` because of
a field-index bug in the probe's own parser (it read `ok.` where it wanted the number); the verdicts come from
the exit codes and from grepping the panic text, both of which were correct, and the bug was fixed in the probe
after the run rather than the run being re-done.

One thing that probe run taught the wrong way: it restores the constant in a `finally`, but the wrapper killed it
with a signal, and a `finally` does not run for an uncaught `SIGTERM`. The tree was left at 25 ms. It was caught
by reading the file, restored, and the restore verified by diffing against a snapshot taken before the probe
started -- the only line that ever differed is the constant itself. A probe that mutates a shipped file needs its
"before" bytes outside the process that mutates it.

== 9. The panic-site ratchet went up by four, and the measurement behind letting it

The full sweep on this tree failed exactly one gate, and it was the gate that should fail:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    ...
    FAIL  panic-site census (exit 1)
        all census fixture checks passed
        xai-grok-test-support: production sites grew [56, 40, 22, 7] -> [56, 40, 26, 7] (unwrap, expect, panic!, unsafe)

        A crate gained a way to panic in code that ships. Remove it, or re-run `scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --write-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv` having decided to keep it.

Four `panic!` in `recv_wait.rs`, and all four are the point of the file:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --list xai-grok-test-support | grep recv_wait
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/recv_wait.rs:93: panic!/unreachable!/todo!/unimplemented!
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/recv_wait.rs:100: panic!/unreachable!/todo!/unimplemented!
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/recv_wait.rs:107: panic!/unreachable!/todo!/unimplemented!
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/recv_wait.rs:177: panic!/unreachable!/todo!/unimplemented!

Those are the oneshot-closed, deadline, channel-closed and broadcast-unavailable paths. The census counts them as
production because they sit outside any `cfg(test)` span, and they have to: the module is compiled into *other*
crates' test builds, where the consumer's `cfg(test)` is not set, so gating it behind `#[cfg(test)]` would stop
all 227 converted call sites from compiling. `docs/audit-followup-report.md` §2.5 already tells a reader to read
this crate's production column as test code wearing production clothes; the ratchet has no such nuance, which is
why the row is raised by a recorded decision rather than quietly.

The decision rests on a measurement, not on that argument. Whether the four panics can reach a binary a user runs
is a question the dependency graph answers:

    $ cargo tree -i xai-grok-test-support -e normal --workspace
    xai-grok-test-support v0.1.0 (/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support)
    └── xai-grok-pager-pty-harness v0.1.0 (/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-pty-harness)

    $ cargo tree -i xai-grok-pager-pty-harness --workspace
    xai-grok-pager-pty-harness v0.1.0 (/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-pty-harness)
    [dev-dependencies]
    └── xai-grok-pager v0.4.2 (/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager)

One non-dev dependent, and that dependent attaches to `xai-grok-pager` only under `[dev-dependencies]`, so the
chain never leaves a test build. Counting the manifests agrees: 18 mentions of the crate name across all
`Cargo.toml` files, 14 of them under `[dev-dependencies]` (13 crates, `xai-grok-pager` twice), and the four
remaining are the workspace member list, the workspace dependency table, that pty-harness `[dependencies]` line,
and the crate's own `[package] name`. No release artifact links any of it.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --check-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
    baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded

The row was edited by hand instead of regenerated, and that is checkable rather than asserted: writing a fresh
baseline to a scratch path and diffing it against the committed file differs in exactly one number, the third
column of the `xai-grok-test-support` row. `--write-baseline` rewrites all 97 rows, so nothing else could have
drifted without showing up in that diff.

    $ cmp {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/panic-baseline-new.tsv scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv && echo IDENTICAL
    IDENTICAL

The committed file is byte-for-byte what `--write-baseline` would write, which is the property that matters: the
next person to regenerate the baseline gets no unrelated churn out of it.

What this does not settle is recorded as a TODO row rather than absorbed: the census decides "production" by file
location (`src/` minus `cfg(test)` spans), while "code that ships" is a statement about the crate graph. Every
future test-support helper that panics loudly raises the same row for the same reason, and the cheapest way for
the next person to silence that noise -- gating the module behind `#[cfg(test)]` -- would break 227 call sites.
The missing piece is crate-level reachability from a shipped target, written up in `docs/audit-followup-report.md`
§2.8.

== 10. A sweep that failed on a test this batch did not touch, and what the runner kept

The second full sweep got past the census and failed a different gate:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    FAIL  cargo test (exit 101)
    ...
        error: 1 target failed:
            `-p xai-tty-utils --lib`
    ...
    FAILED gates: cargo test
    43 gate(s) run, 0 skipped, 1 failed

Which test, the log does not say. `verify-gates.sh` prints the last 40 lines of a failed gate's output and keeps
the rest in a shell variable that dies with the process, and in this run those 40 lines were doctest results from
the crates that come *after* `xai-tty-utils`:

    $ grep -n "FAILED\|panicked\|failures:" {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/sweep-final2.txt
    88:FAILED gates: cargo test

So the transcript named no test at all: the grep for a panic or a failure list returned the summary line and
nothing else, and the only other clue was `error: 1 target failed`. Re-running the same command directly was
green:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast
    EXIT=0

with 0 failures anywhere in the captured output. Nothing in this batch touches `xai-tty-utils`, so the verdict was
"intermittent, load-related, unrelated" -- and that verdict was, at that point, unfalsifiable, because the one
piece of evidence that would have confirmed or refuted it was a test name nobody had.

The name came back from a loop. 30 sequential runs of the suite that had failed, keeping the output of any red
round:

    $ for i in $(seq 1 30); do out=$(RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -q -p xai-tty-utils --lib 2>&1)
      code=$?
      if [ $code -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "ROUND $i FAILED exit=$code"
        echo "$out" | tail -40 > {SCRATCH}/implementer/b24/ttyutils-round-$i.txt
        echo "saved"
      fi
    done
    ROUND 23 FAILED exit=101
    saved
    loop done

    ---- tests::armed_child_dies_when_parent_exits stdout ----
    thread 'tests::armed_child_dies_when_parent_exits' (601006) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-tty-utils/src/lib.rs:1611:9:
    grandchild pid 601059 outlived its parent despite kill_on_parent_death_std (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG not effective)
    ...
    test result: FAILED. 61 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.01s

That is 1 in 30 on an unloaded host running the suite back to back, so a sweep that runs the whole workspace
under full parallelism is expected to hit it occasionally. The test spawns a second copy of itself, that copy
spawns a grandchild armed with `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG`, the intermediate exits, and the driver polls `/proc/<pid>/stat`
for up to **10 s** waiting for the kernel's SIGTERM. 10 s was exceeded, so this is not a budget that is too
tight; the signal genuinely had not arrived, which is why the follow-up row refuses to accept a "just raise the
deadline" answer and asks for the observability fix first.

Nothing here is attributed to this batch, and nothing here is dismissed either: it is recorded as a TODO row with
the name, the line, the message and the measured rate, and the row puts the runner fix (write the failed gate's
full output to a file and name the file in the summary) ahead of the test fix, because a fix aimed at an unnamed
test is a guess.

== 11. What was run, and what was not

Run on the tree as committed: `cargo fmt --all`; `cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked`;
`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`; `cargo test --workspace --locked
--no-fail-fast`, which is where the suites of all eight converted crates run; the guard fixtures (19); the
guard on the shipped tree; the mutation matrix (section 7); `xai-grok-test-support` lib run on its own; and
`xai-grok-shell` lib run on its own (6900 passed, 0 failed, 32 ignored). Also:
`scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py`;
`scripts/ci/classify-open-todos.py --write-export` and `--check-doc`; `scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py
--check-baseline` (section 9); the four reachability commands of section 9; and the full host sweep
`bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build`, three times, because the tree changed between the first two runs and
the second run turned up section 10:

  * run 1, before the panic-baseline decision: `FAILED gates: panic-site census`, `43 gate(s) run, 0 skipped,
    1 failed`;
  * run 2, after it: `FAILED gates: cargo test`, again 43 run / 1 failed, the failure being the `xai-tty-utils`
    flake of section 10 and nothing in this batch;
  * run 3, on the tree as committed:

        $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
        PASS  unbounded test recv
        ...
        PASS  GUI protocol types

        all gates passed on the host (43 run, 0 skipped)
        SWEEP_EXIT=0

The count is 43 where the previous batch recorded 42: `unbounded test recv` is the gate this batch adds, present
in both `ci.yml` and the `gates=()` mirror, and `check-guard-wiring.py` is what keeps those two in step.

Not run here, and stated rather than smoothed over:

  * the probe for the six crates outside `xai-grok-tools` and `xai-grok-shell` (section 8);
  * any fix for the `xai-tty-utils` intermittent failure. It is named, located, measured at 1 in 30 and recorded
    as a TODO row; nothing in this batch touches that crate, and shipping a guess at a fix for a signal that
    arrives late under load would replace a known flake with an unknown one;
  * the root cause of the CI stall in run 37259667663, which this batch does not claim to have found. It removes
    the class of failure that stall belongs to -- after this batch no test-side wait in the workspace can hold a
    job open past 2 s -- and the row that tracks it stays open for the forensic half: a watchdog around the
    `xai-grok-shell` lib suite that captures `gdb -batch -ex 'thread apply all bt'` and then `SIGQUIT`s libtest
    into printing its own line, both uploaded as artifacts.

